# 🎯 CSS Specificity Rules

CSS **Specificity** is an algorithm used by the browser to determine **which CSS rule should be applied** when multiple selectors target the same HTML element.

---

# 📌 Specificity Priority

```text
Inline Style
      ↓
ID Selector
      ↓
Class Selector
Attribute Selector
Pseudo-class
      ↓
Element Selector
Pseudo-element
      ↓
Universal Selector (*)
```

---

# 📊 Specificity Score

| Selector | Specificity Score |
|-----------|-------------------|
| Inline Style | (1,0,0,0) |
| ID Selector | (0,1,0,0) |
| Class Selector | (0,0,1,0) |
| Attribute Selector | (0,0,1,0) |
| Pseudo-class | (0,0,1,0) |
| Element Selector | (0,0,0,1) |
| Pseudo-element | (0,0,0,1) |
| Universal Selector (`*`) | (0,0,0,0) |

---

# 🚀 Browser Decision Flow

```text
Find All Matching CSS Rules
            │
            ▼
Calculate Specificity Score
            │
            ▼
Compare Scores
            │
            ▼
Highest Score Wins
            │
            ▼
If Scores Are Equal
            │
            ▼
Last Written Rule Wins
```

---

# ⚡ Golden Rules

✅ Inline styles have the highest specificity.

✅ ID selectors override Class selectors.

✅ Class selectors override Element selectors.

✅ Element selectors override the Universal selector (`*`).

✅ If two selectors have the same specificity, **the rule written last wins**.

✅ `!important` overrides normal specificity (use it sparingly).

---

# 🏆 Specificity Order

```text
Inline Style
      >
ID Selector
      >
Class / Attribute / Pseudo-class
      >
Element / Pseudo-element
      >
Universal Selector (*)
```

---

# 💡 Best Practices

- Prefer **Class Selectors** for styling.
- Avoid using **ID Selectors** for CSS.
- Avoid using **Inline Styles** except for quick testing.
- Do not overuse **`!important`**.
- Keep selectors simple and maintainable.

---

# 🧠 Remember

> **Higher Specificity Wins.**
>
> If the specificity is the same, **the last declared rule wins.**